Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||05-27-2000||04:24 AM||||people here seem to want to go fast. (duh!) would a 3800 turbo setup similar to a grand national work in our cars? if so, has anyone tried? i have some videos of a GN with about 2000 dollars of mods making a 10.42 quarter mile. 2000 dollars improved the quarter time by more than 4 seconds. imagine what that could do in one of OUR cars! heeee heeeee heeeeeee!||||reg||5
Z||000001||olympic||05-27-2000||04:35 AM||||  I think the GN 3.8 swap would be similar in difficulty to a V8 swap because it also originally designed for front engine, rear wheel drive .  There would be added difficulty in findinng a place to put the intercooler.  Also, the HP produced by a 10 second 3.8 Turbo would shred the Fiero getrag in short order.<BR>   A 3800 SC(from a Bonneville, Park Ave. etc) or even a Northstar swap, with the matching automatic trannys, would be cheaper and more reliable in the long run.||||reg||1
Z||000002||phillip||05-27-2000||06:03 AM||||i hate gm for not making stronger manual fwd trannys. aw well... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||05-27-2000||09:00 AM||||I know a guy who put a GN motor into a front-wheel-drive Buick(like a Chev Celebrity)using the automatic,the soft,stock motor mounts were a problem I guess,surprised some people though.<BR>Was thinking the same thing when I heard about this. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-27-2000||10:38 AM||||I have a small book called "Practical Engine Swapping" that I bought ages ago. In the book, someone asked the question: Can you put a Chrysler 426 hemi in a Honda Civic? Is this a practical swap? The answer is: Yes, you can put a Chrysler hemi in a Honda Civic. Yes, this is a practical swap if you never intend to drive the car. Engine swapping is a fine art. It's especially challenging in a Fiero with it's cramped engine compartment and limited tranny selection.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||05-27-2000||12:12 PM||||It's really all a matter of how far you feel the need to go. With one of the engine cradle  conversions that are available, you are only limited by your imagination. I thought that the Audi 2.8, 30-valve twin turbo v-6 engine and transaxle would fit nicely, since the Audi engine is mounted longitudinally. Dream on!||||reg||1
Z||000006||olympic||05-27-2000||11:46 PM||||  Hey, HOTROD MAGAZINE stuffed a Caddy 501 into a Chevette and managed to drive it down the quarter mile in under 12 seconds.  I guess the sky(or your wallet) really is the limit!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan86gt3.8||05-28-2000||03:05 AM||||yeah I have always wondered about the gn engine into the fiero.  I also thoguht about somebody rebuilding my bottom end and adding a custom turbo.  But 2 things 1.) would there be a tranny that would hold up to the power 2.)There is alott of heat generated by tubo engines and Would wonder if overheating and heat build up in the engine bay would cause you problems.  Anyways I am really interested in doing this someday so keep me informed!||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||05-28-2000||08:36 AM||||Anytime you put a high torque engine (for example my 350 V8 Batmobile) in a Fiero, you have to keep in the back of your mind that if you abuse it, the tranny will sooner or later go bye-bye.<P>But, that's true with any high performance vehicle. I went through a couple M-22 Muncie rebuilds when I was thrashing my old 68 Chevelle SS 396, and rear end rebuilds, etc...<P>As long as you don't do burnouts and other crazy stuff, the Fiero trannies (in all forms) will hold up reasonably well. One thing to remember on an engine swap is that you're mating up a much more powerful motor to a 12-15 year old tranny with who knows how many miles on it. For peace of mind, you can always have the tranny rebuilt at the same time. That'll give you better odds of it lasting longer.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dan86gt3.8||05-29-2000||12:36 PM||||how is your auto holding up batboy?  Also what kind of traction do you get when launching from a dead stop?  I can't spin my wheels for anything it just launches and takes off!  its probably a good thing, my tires will last alott longer now!||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||05-29-2000||06:45 PM||||I can spin the tires, no problem. That's pretty hard on a Fiero tranny though, so I usually don't. So far, the auto tranny's doing ok. However, I really haven't done a lot of driving with it yet.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Dan86gt3.8||05-29-2000||11:53 PM||||so your v8 is not a daily driver?  Are you still working on it?||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||05-30-2000||04:27 PM||||There definitely needs to be a fuse in the drivetrain. Tires are the best and cheapest fuse. I learned this on 2.2L VW's of the past. We would throw big sticky tires on there, drop the clutch and promptly twist the cranks. <P>When I swapped the 3800 supercharged into the Ciera, I considered fatter tires to get traction off the line. After I came to my senses I remembered that something else would give and the only "something else" would be the trans. The CV's and axles are pretty tough although they hate banging. I now run 225/50-16's.<P>Instead, I learned to launch. Other than some known weak points on a trans (ie, my T60E is weakest on a 2-3 shift), the Fiero trans (manual or auto) can handle tons of torque but big sticky tires means something else has to give.<P>IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000013||MikeE||05-31-2000||07:45 PM||||I may be a bit late with this response due to recurring computer probs.  It seems that most members on this list like their fieros with the stock bodies and I concur.  However, if one used a kitcar body with a stretched frame there would probably be room for a longitudinally mounted engine which opens up more trans possibllities.  For a manual, the Porche trans used behind the turbo is very strong, available, and there are a lot of adaptors made.  For an auto the easiest route would be the TH325 & TH425 used in the earlier Cad Eldorados/Olds Toronados.  They have high torque ratings with the early TH425 being the highest.  I don't know if there are adapters made but I suspect there are fron places like mid-engine engineering.  Since making fiberglass panels is not that difficult (but certainly not fun), an enterprising person might mold up a set of panels that kept the Fiero flavor in an extended version.  The result might be akin to something like a midengine Firebird.  Just my .02.||||reg||14
